WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.740
So, what is the simplest and most beautiful triangle in the world, Brady?

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:07.420
(Brady: Ohh...)

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.100
(The Bermuda Triangle?)

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.120
Well...

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.300
[Brady laughs]
- Is it of the, type of triangle that I'm thinking of?

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.000
(Brady: I couldn't think of another famous triangle.)

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.160
(You mean an equilateral triangle.)

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.440
Equilateral triangle. That's what I'm thinking of.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.900
Well, there we go. An equilateral triangle which, inscribed in a circle.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.560
Let us take any other point on this circle.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:35.080
For example, we take a point right here, and we connect it with the vertices of this circle.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.800
Alright, so we're going to label these things b, c and d.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.140
Brady, what do you think could possibly be true about these red segments?

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:53.220
(Brady: It *could* be true, that b and c are the same as d? Added together, or...)

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.920
- Yes.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.940
Amazingly, it is actually true.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:57.900
(Brady: That's true?)

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.680
- Yes! b + c is indeed, equal to d.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.420
But why?
- (Brady: Not "d" factorial?)

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.240
- Not d factorial, definitely, just an exclamation point.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.140
[Chuckles]
(Brady: Okay)

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.540
Now, there are ways to prove this without Ptolemy,

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.480
but since we have worked so hard to prove Ptolemy, why not use it?

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.280
Do we see a quadrilateral?

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.180
(Brady: Yeah!)

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.320
- Absolutely!

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:27.120
I need those green sides, I don't know what they are, but looks like...

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.500
calling them all as is good 'cause this is an equilateral triangle.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.120
So by Ptolemy's theorem, what do we have?

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:43.040
The product of the opposite sides, ab and a,

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:48.620
if I add those products, I must get the product of the diagonals.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:54.800
The green diagonal is a. The red diagonal is d. So we're going to get ad.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:55.960
Hmm!

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.600
Again, we see repetition, let's kill it!

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.820
We divide by a, and we get exactly what we wanted.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.780
The two shorter segments add up to the long one.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:13.700
I remember spending a lot of time on this problem in middle school.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.400
I did not know about Ptolemy's theorem, and I did not know about inversion.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.020
It is possible to do this problem with similar triangles, but they are not all drawn;

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:29.160
you have to come up, in a very imaginative way, with those similar triangles.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.560
So Ptolemy's theorem really wins in terms of elegance and simplicity.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:42.260
Okay. We have now a regular pentagon, or, at least, my version, the closest I could draw by hand here.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.840
Also, it is inscribed in a circle.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:48.860
So what can Ptolemy tell us about this situation?

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:57.280
First of all, we want a quadrilateral, not a pentagon. So we are going to select randomly four of the vertices.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:01.220
Let's say, the bottom four.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.180
We now need to draw the remaining segments between them.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:09.800
Those segments happen to be diagonals of the pentagon.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:21.760
They are all of equal length, because they all participate in these triangles, two of whose sides are green - these are from the pentagon.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.160
At any rate, we need to label things.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.800
Let's say a for the sides of the pentagon,

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.060
and we have two more, but we don't care about them.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:40.800
We label b, all of those diagonals.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.280
There is really nothing else in a pentagon. There are only the sides and the diagonals.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.100
What is their ratio?

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:53.460
If I take the diagonal which looks is the larger of the two and divide it by the side,

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:58.120
which number, do you think, Brady, are we going to get?

00:03:58.120 --> 00:03:59.060
(Brady: I dunno!)

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:00.520
It's a ratio!

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.660
Could be the most famous ratio.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.780
Okay, let's not get ahead of the game. Let's see what happens.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:07.920
(Brady: Alright...)

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:13.260
So Ptolemy tells us about this bottom quadrilateral,

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:19.660
that if I multiply the opposite sides, so a times b,

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:23.780
and the other opposite sides, a times a,

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:29.980
I'm going to get the product of the two diagonals, which is b times b.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.180
Excellent.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.160
Let's put everything on one side:

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:42.060
b squared minus ab minus a squared is equal to zero.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.960
Alright. Any mathematician looking at this says,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.280
"Mmm, this is a homogeneous equation. I can do miracles with it."

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.520
In other words, let's just divide by a squared,

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.180
and see what that would give us.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.520
So b squared over a squared minus...

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.220
- now one of those as will cancel -

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:10.940
...b over a, - 1 'cause a squared divided by a squared is one, and that's zero.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:16.640
Hmm. Well here is the ratio that we were interested in: b over a.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:24.140
If I write this ratio as x, we end up with a quadratic equation,

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:28.760
and when we solve it using the quadratic formula,

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:37.660
we get 1, plus or minus, 1 squared plus 4 times 1, divided by 2

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:42.160
or 1 plus or minus square root of 5 divided by 2.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.780
Mm, wait, hold on- hold on! We still have two choices?!

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:52.400
Is it possible that for some pentagons you get one of those roots and for other pentagons the other root?

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.320
I don't think so.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.880
One of those roots is utterly impossible.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:05.440
The smaller root, which is one minus square root of five over two, is negative.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.260
But the larger root is positive,

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:15.400
and it is the famous Golden Ratio.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:18.740
So what we have shown, using Ptolemy's,

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.660
is that if you take any diagonal of a regular pentagon

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:28.000
and you divide it by any one of its sides, you're going to get the golden ratio.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.680
I think our journey was worth it.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:36.599
(Brady: This video was a continuation of our epic video about the proof of Ptolemy's theorem.)

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:41.640
